      "text": "To find the rate of decay after one minute, you need to use the first-order kinetics equations. Since half-life is constant regardless of concentration, we treat this as a first-order reaction.\n\nHere is the step-by-step process:\n\n### 1. Find the Decay Constant ($\\lambda$)\nThe decay constant represents the probability of decay per unit of time. It is related to the half-life ($t_{1/2}$) by the following formula:\n$$\\lambda \u003d \\frac{\\ln(2)}{t_{1/2}}$$\n\nGiven $t_{1/2} \u003d 4 \\text{ minutes}$:\n$$\\lambda \u003d \\frac{0.6931}{4} \\approx 0.1733 \\text{ min}^{-1}$$\n\n### 2. Determine the Amount Remaining ($N$)\nThe rate of decay depends on how much of the substance is left at that specific moment. The formula for the amount remaining at time $t$ is:\n$$N(t) \u003d N_0 e^{-\\lambda t}$$\n*(Where $N_0$ is the initial amount or concentration).*\n\nAt $t \u003d 1 \\text{ minute}$:\n$$N(1) \u003d N_0 e^{-(0.1733)(1)}$$\n$$N(1) \u003d N_0 e^{-0.1733} \\approx 0.8409 N_0$$\n(This means that after one minute, about 84.1% of your original molecules remain.)\n\n### 3. Calculate the Rate of Decay\nThe **rate of decay** (often called Activity, $R$) at any time $t$ is the decay constant multiplied by the amount of substance present at that time:\n$$\\text{Rate} \u003d \\lambda \\cdot N(t)$$\n\nSubstituting the values we found:\n$$\\text{Rate at 1 min} \u003d 0.1733 \\times (0.8409 N_0)$$\n$$\\text{Rate at 1 min} \\approx 0.1457 \\cdot N_0$$\n\n---\n\n### Summary Formula\nIf you want a single formula to plug in, the rate of decay at time $t$ is:\n$$\\text{Rate} \u003d \\left( \\frac{\\ln(2)}{t_{1/2}} \\right) \\cdot N_0 \\cdot 2^{-(t / t_{1/2})}$$\n\n### Example\nIf you started with **100 grams** of the molecule:\n1.  **Decay constant:** $0.1733$\n2.  **Amount at 1 min:** $100 \\times 0.8409 \u003d 84.09 \\text{ g}$\n3.  **Rate of decay at 1 min:** $0.1733 \\times 84.09 \u003d \\mathbf{14.57 \\text{ g/min}}$\n\n**Note:** If you do not know the initial amount ($N_0$), you can only express the rate as a fraction of the initial amount (e.g., the rate is $14.57\\%$ of the initial quantity per minute).",
