      "text": "Peanuts are a rich source of dietary fiber, but their composition is quite different from \"inulin-heavy\" plants like chicory root or Jerusalem artichokes.\n\nThe fiber in peanuts is primarily **insoluble (approx. 94%)**, with only a small fraction being **soluble (approx. 6%)**. \n\n### 1. Major Insoluble Fibers (The Bulk)\nThe vast majority of peanut fiber consists of structural components that do not dissolve in water. These add bulk to the stool and aid in regular digestion:\n*   **Cellulose:** The most abundant fiber in peanuts. It forms the rigid cell walls of the plant and is completely indigestible by humans, helping move food through the digestive tract.\n*   **Hemicellulose:** Often found alongside cellulose, this fiber also provides structural support to the peanut\u0027s cell walls.\n*   **Lignin:** Technically a non-carbohydrate structural polymer, lignin is often categorized with fiber because it is indigestible and helps maintain the \"crunch\" and structure of the nut.\n\n### 2. Minor Soluble Fibers\nSoluble fibers dissolve in water to form a gel-like substance that can help lower cholesterol and stabilize blood sugar. In peanuts, these include:\n*   **Pectin:** Found in small amounts in the cell walls, pectin is a fermentable fiber that supports gut health.\n*   **Gums and Mucilages:** These are present in trace amounts and contribute to the peanut\u0027s ability to bind water.\n\n### 3. Does Peanut contain Inulin?\n**No, peanuts are not a major source of inulin.**\nWhile inulin is a type of soluble fiber (a fructan) common in many legumes and vegetables, peanuts contain very little of it. In fact, peanuts are classified as **\"Low FODMAP\"** foods precisely because they are low in fructans like inulin. \n*   **Peanut Fiber vs. Inulin:** Peanut fiber is mostly \"bulking\" (insoluble), whereas inulin is \"prebiotic\" (fermentable soluble fiber). If you are looking for inulin, you would find it in high concentrations in chicory root, onions, garlic, and asparagus rather than peanuts.\n\n### 4. Fiber in Different Parts of the Peanut\nThe fiber profile changes depending on which part of the peanut you are looking at:\n*   **The Kernel (The part you eat):** High in cellulose and hemicellulose. Fiber makes up about 8–9% of the total weight of a raw peanut.\n*   **The Skins (The red papery layer):** These are incredibly fiber-dense and also contain high levels of **polyphenols** and **lignin**. Eating peanuts with the skins on significantly increases your fiber and antioxidant intake.\n*   **The Shells:** These are almost entirely fiber (over 60%), consisting mainly of **cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin**. While not edible for humans, they are used industrially as a high-fiber filler for animal feed or as biofuel.\n\n### Summary Table\n| Fiber Type | Solubility | Percentage in Peanuts | Key Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Insoluble** | Low | ~94% | Cellulose, Hemicellulose, Lignin |\n| **Soluble** | High | ~6% | Pectin, Gums |\n| **Inulin** | High | Trace | (Not a primary component) |",
